WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.675
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:09.617
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:17.271
Unit fifteen: Nominal Relative Clauses. Today you will use clauses that act as nouns without an antecedent.

4
00:00:17.571 --> 00:00:20.073
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:24.336
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.183 --> 00:00:25.906
What.

7
00:00:26.917 --> 00:00:28.591
What means the thing that.

8
00:00:29.183 --> 00:00:30.823
What she said surprised me.

9
00:00:31.417 --> 00:00:33.535
Your turn. Say: What.

10
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:36.995
What.

11
00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:38.728
Whoever.

12
00:00:39.717 --> 00:00:41.612
Whoever means any person who.

13
00:00:42.217 --> 00:00:44.100
Whoever calls, take a message.

14
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:47.288
Your turn. Say: Whoever.

15
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:50.995
Whoever.

16
00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:52.681
Whatever.

17
00:00:53.683 --> 00:00:55.509
Whatever means anything that.

18
00:00:56.117 --> 00:00:57.478
Take whatever you need.

19
00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:00.120
Your turn. Say: Whatever.

20
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:03.898
Whatever.

21
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.709
Whichever.

22
00:01:06.717 --> 00:01:08.867
Whichever means any one from a choice.

23
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:11.193
Choose whichever you prefer.

24
00:01:11.783 --> 00:01:14.087
Your turn. Say: Whichever.

25
00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:17.830
Whichever.

26
00:01:18.583 --> 00:01:20.118
Free relative clause.

27
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:24.695
Free relative clause means a relative clause with no antecedent.

28
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:27.097
A free relative clause.

29
00:01:27.683 --> 00:01:30.742
Your turn. Say: Free relative clause.

30
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.414
Free relative clause.

31
00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.720
No antecedent.

32
00:01:38.717 --> 00:01:41.436
No antecedent means no noun before the pronoun.

33
00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:43.991
No antecedent is needed.

34
00:01:44.583 --> 00:01:47.468
Your turn. Say: No antecedent.

35
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:51.799
No antecedent.

36
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:53.795
Embedded meaning.

37
00:01:54.783 --> 00:01:58.617
Embedded meaning means a meaning inside a larger sentence.

38
00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:00.554
Embedded meaning.

39
00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:04.220
Your turn. Say: Embedded meaning.

40
00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:08.288
Embedded meaning.

41
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:10.492
Flexible reference.

42
00:02:11.483 --> 00:02:14.806
Flexible reference means a pronoun that can point to anything.

43
00:02:15.417 --> 00:02:16.812
Flexible reference.

44
00:02:17.417 --> 00:02:20.324
Your turn. Say: Flexible reference.

45
00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:24.760
Flexible reference.

46
00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:29.566
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:33.879
Visal, what is the grammar of What she said surprised me?

48
00:02:34.983 --> 00:02:45.031
What she said is a nominal relative clause. It acts like a noun phrase, meaning the thing that she said, and it is the subject of surprised.

49
00:02:46.117 --> 00:02:47.536
Which words begin them?

50
00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:58.134
What, whoever, whatever, whichever, whenever, wherever. Whoever wins gets a prize. Take whatever you need. Choose whichever suits you.

51
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.563
How do they differ from ordinary relatives?

52
00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:19.074
An ordinary relative follows a noun: the book that I read. A nominal relative contains its own antecedent, so we say What I read, not The what I read. Also, -ever adds the idea of any or no matter which.

53
00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:21.943
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:22.783 --> 00:03:29.044
Grammar focus: Nominal relative clause: what and whoever and whatever plus clause = noun phrase.

55
00:03:29.344 --> 00:03:30.391
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.083 --> 00:03:32.758
What she said surprised me.

57
00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:35.499
Whoever wins gets the prize.

58
00:03:36.383 --> 00:03:37.768
Take whatever you need.

59
00:03:38.733 --> 00:03:44.497
Nominal relative clauses start with what or -ever words and act as nouns. They need no antecedent.

60
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:48.076
Be careful! Don't say: Take what ever you need.

61
00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:51.998
Say: Take whatever you need.

62
00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:53.938
Whatever is one word.

63
00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:57.316
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:58.100 --> 00:03:59.461
Take Whatever You Need.

65
00:03:59.761 --> 00:04:02.585
What the teacher explained made everything clear.

66
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.168
Whoever finishes first may leave early.

67
00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:09.039
Take whichever book you prefer.

68
00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:12.718
Whatever you decide, I will support you.

69
00:04:13.733 --> 00:04:16.406
These clauses act like nouns inside the sentence.

70
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:21.664
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:22.517 --> 00:04:24.272
blank she said surprised me.

72
00:04:24.572 --> 00:04:27.680
A, What. B, That. C, Which.

73
00:04:31.233 --> 00:04:33.047
The answer is A: What.

74
00:04:34.183 --> 00:04:35.986
blank wins gets the prize.

75
00:04:36.286 --> 00:04:39.834
A, Whatever. B, Whoever. C, Whichever.

76
00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.492
The answer is B: Whoever.

77
00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:49.741
Choose blank you prefer, the red one or the blue one.

78
00:04:50.041 --> 00:04:53.427
A, whichever. B, whoever. C, whenever.

79
00:04:56.967 --> 00:04:58.827
The answer is A: whichever.

80
00:04:59.983 --> 00:05:02.436
A nominal relative clause has no blank.

81
00:05:02.736 --> 00:05:06.679
A, verb. B, antecedent. C, subject.

82
00:05:10.233 --> 00:05:12.488
The answer is B: antecedent.

83
00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.938
According to the reading, what may the early finisher do?

84
00:05:17.238 --> 00:05:20.485
A, leave early. B, sing. C, sleep.

85
00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:26.486
The answer is A: leave early.

86
00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.979
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:31.817 --> 00:05:35.314
Write a sentence using 'whatever' as a nominal relative clause.

88
00:05:44.117 --> 00:05:45.652
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:45.952 --> 00:05:59.172
I will accept whatever you decide. Whatever happens tomorrow, we will face it together. The clause whatever you decide acts as the object of accept, so it works like a noun.

90
00:05:59.472 --> 00:06:01.181
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:02.083 --> 00:06:09.949
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and nominal relative clause: what and whoever and whatever plus clause = noun phrase.

92
00:06:10.249 --> 00:06:15.908
Today's value is open-mindedness. Be open to new places, people and ideas.

93
00:06:16.208 --> 00:06:19.023
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:19.323 --> 00:06:22.126
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

